WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:06.300
Hi, everyone.

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:07.740
Welcome.

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:11.850
So we're going to be onboarding
on our journey for the Class

00:00:11.850 --> 00:00:15.750
E274 on data compression
theory and applications.

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:18.330
And today it's all going
to be about introduction

00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:20.550
and logistics and
then we'll just deep

00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:24.030
dive into the material
towards the second half.

00:00:24.030 --> 00:00:26.010
So this is your course staff.

00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:30.780
We have Professor Tsachy
Weissman sitting right there

00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:33.030
in the cool jacket.

00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:35.460
We have Shubham
who is also there.

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:38.830
And he'll be taking the second
half of today's lecture.

00:00:38.830 --> 00:00:42.450
I'm Pulkit and I'll
also be a part lecturer.

00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:45.900
And then finally we have our
awesome TA sitting there so

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:48.810
for any class related material.

00:00:48.810 --> 00:00:52.080
We also have Kedhar who was the
instructor from last year, who

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:53.760
will be helping us
from the sidelines.

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:58.820
So you have another extra
resource at your disposal.

00:00:58.820 --> 00:01:01.990
So let's just dive in.

00:01:01.990 --> 00:01:05.080
So this is a curve which
you might encounter

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:07.090
at some point in your life.

00:01:07.090 --> 00:01:10.630
The y-axis here is the
data volume in zettabytes,

00:01:10.630 --> 00:01:12.250
and the x-axis is the year.

00:01:12.250 --> 00:01:14.890
So this is the volume of
data which has been created

00:01:14.890 --> 00:01:17.830
and replicated worldwide.

00:01:17.830 --> 00:01:20.110
And it has a trend which is--

00:01:20.110 --> 00:01:24.280
it's exponentially increasing.

00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:28.810
And this curve shows up in
all sorts of applications.

00:01:28.810 --> 00:01:32.200
But what does these
numbers really mean?

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:33.730
What do they reflect?

00:01:33.730 --> 00:01:36.910
More precisely, what
exactly is a zettabyte?

00:01:36.910 --> 00:01:38.470
Why should I care?

00:01:38.470 --> 00:01:43.450
So let's try to get some
sense of these numbers.

00:01:43.450 --> 00:01:45.340
So let's start with a megabyte.

00:01:45.340 --> 00:01:48.760
So megabyte really is
zeros behind that one.

00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:50.560
And it's roughly
the space which is

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:53.320
occupied by a lossless
image in your smartphone.

00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:56.200
So this is the image
of my cousin's dog Ace.

00:01:56.200 --> 00:01:59.500
And the unfiltered version of
it was taking approximately

00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:01.225
a megabyte on my phone.

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:07.800
Continuing, as you add
three more zeros behind it,

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:11.880
you get a gigabyte which is
approximately the space which

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:15.300
is occupied by a typical
4k video of a few minutes

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:16.530
duration.

00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:19.680
And this was supposed to stream
automatically from YouTube

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:23.370
but it was basically a video
clip of your favorite anime.

00:02:27.530 --> 00:02:31.400
Continuing, if you add
three more zeros behind it,

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:34.460
now we start talking
about terabytes.

00:02:34.460 --> 00:02:37.490
And this is something which I'm
sure at some point you might

00:02:37.490 --> 00:02:39.140
have encountered
when you went on

00:02:39.140 --> 00:02:43.970
to buy an electronic device
from your favorite manufacturer.

00:02:43.970 --> 00:02:47.660
This is what that
manufacturer charges you

00:02:47.660 --> 00:02:50.990
for each extra terabyte storage.

00:02:50.990 --> 00:02:54.050
And you need that extra
terabyte storage year after year

00:02:54.050 --> 00:02:55.580
to store all those
photos and videos

00:02:55.580 --> 00:02:57.980
which we just talked about.

00:02:57.980 --> 00:03:01.820
So at this point with
the, 12 zeros behind,

00:03:01.820 --> 00:03:03.860
we are already at
the typical storage

00:03:03.860 --> 00:03:06.350
which is available on
your laptops as of now.

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:10.080
Let's continue.

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:12.090
Add another three zeros.

00:03:12.090 --> 00:03:14.730
And what we get is
actually a petabyte.

00:03:14.730 --> 00:03:16.980
And at this scale
we are now talking

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:18.870
about the databases
which are stored

00:03:18.870 --> 00:03:22.590
by really even medium
scale companies as

00:03:22.590 --> 00:03:23.610
of today standards.

00:03:23.610 --> 00:03:26.940
It's actually the de
facto Cloud storage unit.

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:33.060
So if you have ever heard of
AWS S3, Google storage, Azure,

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:34.830
all of these are
cloud storage services

00:03:34.830 --> 00:03:36.270
where you can store your data.

00:03:36.270 --> 00:03:38.843
And when you talk
about how much data

00:03:38.843 --> 00:03:40.260
is being stored
by these companies

00:03:40.260 --> 00:03:42.750
you're typically talking
units of petabytes.

00:03:42.750 --> 00:03:45.540
And again a petabyte,
at each of these steps,

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:48.600
we have been adding
three orders of magnitude

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:51.480
behind what we have
been seeing before.

00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:54.720
In fact, a cool fact,
Meta was roughly

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:56.880
generating more than
a petabyte of data

00:03:56.880 --> 00:03:58.470
to be ingested every day.

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:04.680
And so at this scale, basically
what you're talking about

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:07.660
is millions of
dollars of management

00:04:07.660 --> 00:04:10.510
if you are a business person
from a company managing

00:04:10.510 --> 00:04:11.260
this data.

00:04:11.260 --> 00:04:16.720
Or if you are a team
at AWS S3 or GCS,

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:18.670
by managing petabytes
of data, you

00:04:18.670 --> 00:04:21.820
are talking about millions of
dollars of data in management

00:04:21.820 --> 00:04:22.615
under your control.

00:04:27.530 --> 00:04:30.550
So far so good.

00:04:30.550 --> 00:04:34.660
Let's add another three
zeros, because why not.

00:04:34.660 --> 00:04:37.960
So now we are at one, two,
three, four, five, six.

00:04:37.960 --> 00:04:40.840
Six times three zeros behind
the one of the bytes which

00:04:40.840 --> 00:04:41.950
we were talking about.

00:04:41.950 --> 00:04:44.110
And what you get is an exabyte.

00:04:44.110 --> 00:04:46.600
And this is roughly
the estimate of data

00:04:46.600 --> 00:04:49.780
which is being transferred
over the internet daily.

00:04:49.780 --> 00:04:53.080
Every day, this is
the amount of bytes

00:04:53.080 --> 00:04:55.300
you are sending to and back.

00:04:59.030 --> 00:05:02.840
And this finally leads us to
what this number zettabyte

00:05:02.840 --> 00:05:04.730
really is.

00:05:04.730 --> 00:05:07.130
One zettabyte is
actually three orders

00:05:07.130 --> 00:05:11.320
of magnitude over what I just
talked about the last thing.

00:05:11.320 --> 00:05:13.310
So I want to wait
a second and I want

00:05:13.310 --> 00:05:14.560
you guys to really appreciate.

00:05:14.560 --> 00:05:16.570
These are 21 zeros.

00:05:16.570 --> 00:05:20.560
We started with just six zeros
behind one of your photos.

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:23.620
It's not multiplied by
two, it's multiplied

00:05:23.620 --> 00:05:27.310
by 10 raised to 15 from
that single photo which

00:05:27.310 --> 00:05:31.870
you are very carefully
to capture on your phone.

00:05:31.870 --> 00:05:36.910
And this zettabyte is what's
being shown on this curve.

00:05:36.910 --> 00:05:40.900
So when you went on from, let's
say two zettabyte around 2010

00:05:40.900 --> 00:05:44.050
to something like 64
zettabytes, it's actually

00:05:44.050 --> 00:05:47.470
62 of 21 zeros of data
added in the world.

00:05:50.250 --> 00:05:53.430
So clearly managing this much
amount of data is an issue.

00:05:53.430 --> 00:05:55.650
Data is growing at
an exponential scale

00:05:55.650 --> 00:05:59.100
and this is a serious
problem in all applications.

00:05:59.100 --> 00:06:00.720
You have to somehow
manage this data.

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:02.430
You have to somehow
understand this data.

00:06:02.430 --> 00:06:05.370
You have to use this data
to drive your business.

00:06:05.370 --> 00:06:07.050
Or you have to use
this data to really

00:06:07.050 --> 00:06:09.180
come up with the cool
Machine Learning applications

00:06:09.180 --> 00:06:12.740
if that's what you care about.

00:06:12.740 --> 00:06:15.650
So this clearly says
that there is a need.

00:06:15.650 --> 00:06:21.230
If this continues, we won't
just have enough space

00:06:21.230 --> 00:06:24.230
to store data for ourselves.

00:06:24.230 --> 00:06:27.035
So you have to worry about
the storage at this point.

00:06:30.990 --> 00:06:35.160
But interestingly, it's not
just about data at rest,

00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:38.040
it's not about just,
I collect this data,

00:06:38.040 --> 00:06:41.760
I store it at one place and
life is great if I can do that.

00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:44.250
It's also about
the data in motion.

00:06:44.250 --> 00:06:46.260
Once you store this
data, you will use it

00:06:46.260 --> 00:06:48.410
for 1,000 different
applications for analytics,

00:06:48.410 --> 00:06:50.160
for just transferring
data to your friends

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:52.770
so on and so forth.

00:06:52.770 --> 00:06:55.020
And that's also
a very big issue,

00:06:55.020 --> 00:06:56.970
you have to move
this data around.

00:06:56.970 --> 00:07:00.090
So this is like an estimate
of like the proportion

00:07:00.090 --> 00:07:03.780
of internet data
traffic around 2021.

00:07:03.780 --> 00:07:06.930
And here what you see
interestingly is videos

00:07:06.930 --> 00:07:12.960
are taking roughly 50% of your
internet data traffic daily.

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:15.690
And this is a serious issue.

00:07:15.690 --> 00:07:17.370
How can I convince you that?

00:07:17.370 --> 00:07:23.460
Back during COVID, Netflix got a
technical request legal request

00:07:23.460 --> 00:07:27.640
from EU to reduce its bandwidth
because they were just

00:07:27.640 --> 00:07:29.093
not able to support it.

00:07:29.093 --> 00:07:31.510
They were not able to support
the increase in data traffic

00:07:31.510 --> 00:07:33.560
around their networks.

00:07:33.560 --> 00:07:36.820
So Netflix was basically asked
to either reduce the quality

00:07:36.820 --> 00:07:39.018
or improve their
compression algorithms,

00:07:39.018 --> 00:07:40.810
improve the compression
which they provide.

00:07:44.560 --> 00:07:47.470
And again, these are just
some conservative numbers

00:07:47.470 --> 00:07:49.137
of what you are
really streaming when

00:07:49.137 --> 00:07:51.220
you are watching your
favorite show, how much data

00:07:51.220 --> 00:07:55.210
you are consuming without
even thinking twice about it.

00:07:55.210 --> 00:07:58.060
So these numbers, if
you just look at them

00:07:58.060 --> 00:08:01.930
like high definition Netflix
stream, can use as much as 3gb

00:08:01.930 --> 00:08:04.240
of data each hour.

00:08:04.240 --> 00:08:06.910
These numbers and the
world which we live in

00:08:06.910 --> 00:08:09.970
is unprecedented and it's
an exponential growth

00:08:09.970 --> 00:08:11.470
scale with respect to the data.

00:08:14.440 --> 00:08:17.200
Great.

00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:21.970
But the interesting part is I
said about videos and Netflix.

00:08:21.970 --> 00:08:24.070
But it's not just about videos.

00:08:24.070 --> 00:08:26.770
It's not about any
single kind of data.

00:08:26.770 --> 00:08:29.770
Data compression shows
up and is of value

00:08:29.770 --> 00:08:32.338
wherever you collect data
and whatever kind of data

00:08:32.338 --> 00:08:33.130
you are collecting.

00:08:33.130 --> 00:08:37.450
So things like text,
log files, code.

00:08:37.450 --> 00:08:42.220
Even if you do-- if you ever
use Github and you do a git

00:08:42.220 --> 00:08:44.620
pull this is like a standard
message which comes up.

00:08:44.620 --> 00:08:46.300
So this is, for
example, an instance

00:08:46.300 --> 00:08:49.210
of when I was updating the
website for the course.

00:08:49.210 --> 00:08:51.760
And very interestingly
here it says something

00:08:51.760 --> 00:08:55.390
like compressing objects.

00:08:55.390 --> 00:08:57.910
So if you have never ever
actually looked at it

00:08:57.910 --> 00:08:59.770
carefully, even
Github every time you

00:08:59.770 --> 00:09:02.380
are doing a pull push
any of those things

00:09:02.380 --> 00:09:04.240
you have to compress.

00:09:04.240 --> 00:09:06.850
Otherwise you can't really
do a version management,

00:09:06.850 --> 00:09:09.550
you just can't creating copies
at every single change which

00:09:09.550 --> 00:09:13.180
you are making and committing
to your favorite repo.

00:09:13.180 --> 00:09:15.490
So this is like working
in the background.

00:09:15.490 --> 00:09:16.600
But not only that.

00:09:16.600 --> 00:09:19.930
There are genomic data, emails,
all your Google searches,

00:09:19.930 --> 00:09:23.770
tweets, images,
videos, things like EEG

00:09:23.770 --> 00:09:28.960
which is electron
encephalogram, sensor data.

00:09:28.960 --> 00:09:31.630
If you think about the
biomedical applications,

00:09:31.630 --> 00:09:34.420
the amount of data you're
collecting is immense.

00:09:34.420 --> 00:09:37.780
And again there is
many times there is--

00:09:37.780 --> 00:09:40.630
it's not even possible
to transmit this data,

00:09:40.630 --> 00:09:41.980
collect this data.

00:09:41.980 --> 00:09:47.460
Let alone do any
analysis on top of it.

00:09:47.460 --> 00:09:50.310
Neural recordings,
your multiverse.

00:09:50.310 --> 00:09:53.400
And not even that, even
LLMs and ML models.

00:09:53.400 --> 00:09:56.190
So if you care about
Machine Learning,

00:09:56.190 --> 00:09:57.870
there is a big
push for machine--

00:09:57.870 --> 00:10:00.390
there is a deeper connection
between compression and Machine

00:10:00.390 --> 00:10:01.470
Learning models.

00:10:01.470 --> 00:10:04.728
And there is bidirectional
communication in the sense

00:10:04.728 --> 00:10:07.020
like people have been using
Machine Learning to improve

00:10:07.020 --> 00:10:07.740
compression.

00:10:07.740 --> 00:10:10.320
But not only that, compression
has been an integral part

00:10:10.320 --> 00:10:12.900
to understand what's
happening in Machine Learning.

00:10:12.900 --> 00:10:15.660
So if you have if you are aware
of what's happening of, maybe I

00:10:15.660 --> 00:10:17.820
don't know, Lama and so
on and so forth, you would

00:10:17.820 --> 00:10:20.790
also there are all these
sorts of quantize models.

00:10:20.790 --> 00:10:22.380
Why do you need these
quantize models?

00:10:22.380 --> 00:10:24.090
Why is everyone after them.

00:10:24.090 --> 00:10:26.753
Because unless you do that you
can't run any of these things

00:10:26.753 --> 00:10:27.795
on your favorite devices.

00:10:30.560 --> 00:10:32.300
So what I really want
to highlight here

00:10:32.300 --> 00:10:34.490
is it's omnipresent.

00:10:34.490 --> 00:10:37.340
No matter what's your
application area, maybe

00:10:37.340 --> 00:10:39.020
you are a bioengineer.

00:10:39.020 --> 00:10:41.360
Maybe you don't care you're
just a software engineer

00:10:41.360 --> 00:10:44.150
and wants to have the
most efficient version

00:10:44.150 --> 00:10:45.950
management for your softwares.

00:10:45.950 --> 00:10:49.460
Or you're just a machine
learning engineer.

00:10:49.460 --> 00:10:54.140
No matter what you do, you
will see compression at play

00:10:54.140 --> 00:10:57.440
at some point during
your lifetime.

00:11:01.410 --> 00:11:05.160
Even more interestingly,
your compression,

00:11:05.160 --> 00:11:07.110
it's filled with trade offs.

00:11:07.110 --> 00:11:10.710
It's not really when
Github says, compressed.

00:11:10.710 --> 00:11:12.810
There were a lot
of design decisions

00:11:12.810 --> 00:11:15.510
which were made
in the background.

00:11:15.510 --> 00:11:17.160
And what we'll teach
you in this class

00:11:17.160 --> 00:11:20.310
is also to understand how to
make these design decisions.

00:11:20.310 --> 00:11:24.210
It's not just about taking
a file, right clicking,

00:11:24.210 --> 00:11:27.070
compress, and be done.

00:11:27.070 --> 00:11:30.580
It's always about the trade offs
given your applications, what

00:11:30.580 --> 00:11:31.990
you really want to achieve.

00:11:31.990 --> 00:11:35.110
And this is a curve which
we'll see a lot in the--

00:11:35.110 --> 00:11:38.170
variants of it a lot in
the second half and Tsachy

00:11:38.170 --> 00:11:40.780
will cover its
theoretical aspects too.

00:11:40.780 --> 00:11:44.260
But basically what I'm showing
here on the x-axis is size,

00:11:44.260 --> 00:11:46.480
and on the y-axis is error.

00:11:46.480 --> 00:11:48.520
And for those of who--

00:11:48.520 --> 00:11:51.575
those of you who are aware,
it's like rate distortion curve

00:11:51.575 --> 00:11:53.950
if you would have heard of it
at some point in your life.

00:11:53.950 --> 00:11:57.280
And the basic idea is this
that when you have higher size,

00:11:57.280 --> 00:12:00.220
you can get away with
smaller errors in your files.

00:12:00.220 --> 00:12:02.170
And as you keep
decreasing your size,

00:12:02.170 --> 00:12:05.080
you will have higher
and higher errors.

00:12:05.080 --> 00:12:08.530
And this leads to some very
interesting trade offs.

00:12:08.530 --> 00:12:10.810
And maybe let's get
into some of them.

00:12:10.810 --> 00:12:14.900
Let's try if this works or not.

00:12:14.900 --> 00:12:17.450
So now-- hello.

