00:16:56.170 --> 00:16:58.958
And during the class, we'll
get into such details.

00:16:58.958 --> 00:17:01.000
Why these things happen,
what are the compressors

00:17:01.000 --> 00:17:03.970
being used, when you can throw
away stuff without introducing

00:17:03.970 --> 00:17:05.260
a lot of distortions.

00:17:05.260 --> 00:17:08.740
And this is basically the job
of all the big businesses.

00:17:08.740 --> 00:17:10.210
Like Netflix
optimizes its streams

00:17:10.210 --> 00:17:11.710
so that you can get
the best quality

00:17:11.710 --> 00:17:14.560
or your favorite
streaming provider.

00:17:14.560 --> 00:17:15.760
Same with Spotify.

00:17:15.760 --> 00:17:17.260
If you look at
Spotify settings, you

00:17:17.260 --> 00:17:19.930
would see whether you want the
highest quality or the best

00:17:19.930 --> 00:17:20.890
data saving mode.

00:17:20.890 --> 00:17:25.470
This is the curve along which
they are basically playing.

00:17:25.470 --> 00:17:27.450
So hopefully, by
the end of the class

00:17:27.450 --> 00:17:30.720
you can appreciate all these
things better technically.

00:17:30.720 --> 00:17:34.890
And be able to understand these
trade offs more intricately.

00:17:38.590 --> 00:17:39.700
So I'll wait.

00:17:39.700 --> 00:17:40.990
Any questions so far?

00:17:44.360 --> 00:17:44.860
Cool.

00:17:49.660 --> 00:17:52.150
So this is really the punchline.

00:17:52.150 --> 00:17:56.260
And I'm quoting Saki who is
just sitting right behind.

00:17:56.260 --> 00:17:58.450
Because this is where
I heard it first from.

00:17:58.450 --> 00:18:01.300
And I really think this
line summarizes the data

00:18:01.300 --> 00:18:02.140
compression.

00:18:02.140 --> 00:18:05.620
Which is, it's basically
the succinct representation

00:18:05.620 --> 00:18:07.780
of information.

00:18:07.780 --> 00:18:10.180
So you're OK to throw
away stuff, as long

00:18:10.180 --> 00:18:12.100
as you don't throw
away information.

00:18:12.100 --> 00:18:14.860
Or you try to keep basically
whatever message being

00:18:14.860 --> 00:18:17.890
communicated through
whichever medium as close as

00:18:17.890 --> 00:18:19.450
possible to the original one.

00:18:23.550 --> 00:18:26.040
And like we have
been saying, why

00:18:26.040 --> 00:18:27.330
do we care about compression?

00:18:27.330 --> 00:18:28.920
I think I'll go
through this fast

00:18:28.920 --> 00:18:32.400
because I did show you guys
some examples of why you might

00:18:32.400 --> 00:18:34.290
care about the compression.

00:18:34.290 --> 00:18:37.560
But basic point points
have remained the same so

00:18:37.560 --> 00:18:39.030
like storage is costly.

00:18:39.030 --> 00:18:41.575
And we are generating
exponentially exabytes more

00:18:41.575 --> 00:18:42.075
data.

00:18:44.580 --> 00:18:49.080
More so, it is really purest
form of information processing.

00:18:49.080 --> 00:18:54.930
Compression integrally
is related to your signal

00:18:54.930 --> 00:18:56.160
denoising in a way.

00:18:56.160 --> 00:18:59.130
You are basically trying to
keep the most useful part

00:18:59.130 --> 00:19:01.950
of the information, and whatever
thing you are outputting out

00:19:01.950 --> 00:19:04.140
of this compression.

00:19:04.140 --> 00:19:08.730
And in same way it's equivalent
to communication without noise.

00:19:08.730 --> 00:19:10.860
So when you are
communicating, maybe you

00:19:10.860 --> 00:19:11.880
are adding some noise.

00:19:11.880 --> 00:19:16.350
You are spending some storage,
some bits to actually spend

00:19:16.350 --> 00:19:17.280
on those noise.

00:19:17.280 --> 00:19:19.530
And what compression
tries to do,

00:19:19.530 --> 00:19:23.070
is extract the relevant
information out of it.

00:19:26.470 --> 00:19:29.620
So you also have these
compressed representations,

00:19:29.620 --> 00:19:32.740
which might be very easy
to transmit and search on.

00:19:32.740 --> 00:19:34.960
Because now you have
thrown away the fluff

00:19:34.960 --> 00:19:38.050
and kept really the
information part of the things.

00:19:38.050 --> 00:19:42.280
And it can even simplify
implementations.

00:19:42.280 --> 00:19:44.140
For example, if you
are working on just

00:19:44.140 --> 00:19:48.100
the compressed representation
instead of the whole big data.

00:19:48.100 --> 00:19:50.980
You may not be able to move
that whole big data versus

00:19:50.980 --> 00:19:53.110
if you are working with
a smaller part of it.

00:19:53.110 --> 00:19:54.910
Maybe you can do
something about it.

00:19:58.140 --> 00:20:02.160
More so, it has a very
fundamental relation

00:20:02.160 --> 00:20:06.390
to the data modeling,
prediction, and even

00:20:06.390 --> 00:20:09.870
your favorite ML, so as to say.

00:20:09.870 --> 00:20:11.520
And this is a point
which we'll try

00:20:11.520 --> 00:20:12.780
to drive home at some point.

00:20:12.780 --> 00:20:16.620
That basically a good compressor
is a very good predictor,

00:20:16.620 --> 00:20:18.060
and vice versa.

00:20:18.060 --> 00:20:20.040
If you basically somehow
are able to come up

00:20:20.040 --> 00:20:22.110
with a good predictor,
you can use it

00:20:22.110 --> 00:20:25.620
for compression and some
conditions which we'll talk

00:20:25.620 --> 00:20:28.860
about, and so on and so forth.

00:20:28.860 --> 00:20:30.810
So it's very
fundamentally linked

00:20:30.810 --> 00:20:34.290
to what you are able to predict.

00:20:34.290 --> 00:20:38.100
And finally it is really
a very critical building

00:20:38.100 --> 00:20:43.710
block in today's, I would say
any scalable and efficient

00:20:43.710 --> 00:20:45.840
system.

00:20:45.840 --> 00:20:48.690
In fact, my first
fall into compression

00:20:48.690 --> 00:20:50.910
wasn't even through the
standard compression thing.

00:20:50.910 --> 00:20:54.720
I was trying to build an
application for brain machine

00:20:54.720 --> 00:20:55.650
interfaces.

00:20:55.650 --> 00:20:58.140
And there was
absolutely no way I

00:20:58.140 --> 00:21:01.650
could have collected that
data and transmitted it.

00:21:01.650 --> 00:21:03.570
It was just impossible.

00:21:03.570 --> 00:21:06.390
I was working on
some eye prosthesis

00:21:06.390 --> 00:21:08.532
and I would have burned the eye.

00:21:08.532 --> 00:21:10.740
Way before I would have been
able to collect the data

00:21:10.740 --> 00:21:13.290
I would need.

00:21:13.290 --> 00:21:15.270
So what I'm really
trying to highlight is,

00:21:15.270 --> 00:21:20.190
no matter what application
you are into, at some point,

00:21:20.190 --> 00:21:24.270
you might run into a
compression just as a necessity.

00:21:24.270 --> 00:21:28.200
Or we have actually sitting
in the class and in her case,

00:21:28.200 --> 00:21:32.970
she used compression as a
tool for her application.

00:21:32.970 --> 00:21:35.850
And that's another way
where you might come up

00:21:35.850 --> 00:21:37.260
with some of the
tools we'll talk

00:21:37.260 --> 00:21:39.818
in this-- you might use some
of the tools which we might

00:21:39.818 --> 00:21:41.610
come in this class in
very interesting ways

00:21:41.610 --> 00:21:42.810
in your application.

00:21:42.810 --> 00:21:45.270
And that all stems back
to all of these things.

00:21:45.270 --> 00:21:48.090
That essentially, it has very
deep connection to prediction.

00:21:48.090 --> 00:21:52.660
It is basically the purest
form of information processing.

00:21:52.660 --> 00:21:54.880
So compression is basically
what I want to highlight.

00:21:54.880 --> 00:21:55.870
It's way beyond.

00:21:55.870 --> 00:21:59.200
Again, right click,
compress, zip, done.

00:21:59.200 --> 00:22:00.820
It has it has--

00:22:00.820 --> 00:22:03.130
it is very interesting.

00:22:03.130 --> 00:22:05.830
In fact, it is so interesting.

00:22:05.830 --> 00:22:07.360
Actually, let me pause here.

00:22:07.360 --> 00:22:08.710
Any questions so far?

00:22:15.270 --> 00:22:18.150
So compression is super
interesting, no matter what

00:22:18.150 --> 00:22:20.580
your interests are.

00:22:20.580 --> 00:22:23.100
So what I want to
highlight is, that there

00:22:23.100 --> 00:22:26.070
is something for everyone
who is sitting here.

00:22:26.070 --> 00:22:29.280
You love theory, you really
like to understand the world

00:22:29.280 --> 00:22:31.410
in mathematical formulations.

00:22:31.410 --> 00:22:33.630
Compression has very
deep connections

00:22:33.630 --> 00:22:35.160
to information theory.

00:22:35.160 --> 00:22:36.540
And here it's
Claude Shannon, who

00:22:36.540 --> 00:22:40.650
is the father of
information theory.

00:22:40.650 --> 00:22:43.440
For example, I started
this presentation

00:22:43.440 --> 00:22:46.308
with exabytes, megabyte,
kilo whatever terabyte,

00:22:46.308 --> 00:22:47.100
so on and so forth.

00:22:47.100 --> 00:22:49.110
And all of you are like,
yeah, we understand.

00:22:49.110 --> 00:22:50.970
Very cool.

00:22:50.970 --> 00:22:51.540
Sure.

00:22:51.540 --> 00:22:53.190
So we are in a digital
information age.

00:22:53.190 --> 00:22:56.040
Everybody knows what
bits are, what bytes are.

00:22:56.040 --> 00:22:59.190
But basically, it has a
very fundamental connection

00:22:59.190 --> 00:23:00.630
to whatever we have been doing.

00:23:00.630 --> 00:23:02.940
It has fundamental
connection to information.

00:23:02.940 --> 00:23:06.150
And it's not
coincidental or arbitrary

00:23:06.150 --> 00:23:08.490
that we are talking
in terms of bytes.

00:23:08.490 --> 00:23:11.250
And in fact, we'll cover
the basics in this course

00:23:11.250 --> 00:23:12.000
next week.

00:23:12.000 --> 00:23:13.540
Most likely, you
will start seeing

00:23:13.540 --> 00:23:17.147
the exact bits coming up.

00:23:17.147 --> 00:23:18.730
But if you are more
interested I would

00:23:18.730 --> 00:23:22.240
encourage you guys to take E276,
which is a specific information

00:23:22.240 --> 00:23:22.840
theory course.

00:23:22.840 --> 00:23:24.940
Again, taught by Saki.

00:23:27.450 --> 00:23:28.710
Another interesting question.

00:23:28.710 --> 00:23:30.870
So take your
favorite compressor,

00:23:30.870 --> 00:23:33.840
I don't know whichever
one you want to pick.

00:23:33.840 --> 00:23:34.890
Input data file.

00:23:37.467 --> 00:23:39.300
I don't know if you
have ever thought of it.

00:23:39.300 --> 00:23:43.170
But why can't I just keep
applying it in a loop.

00:23:43.170 --> 00:23:46.170
Whatever output I get out
of, I don't know, zip.

00:23:46.170 --> 00:23:50.160
Let me zip it again, zip
it again, zip it again?

00:23:50.160 --> 00:23:52.560
Why am I not able
to get to 0 bytes?

00:23:52.560 --> 00:23:53.955
where am I taking any space?

00:23:58.140 --> 00:24:02.250
So actually very interestingly,
apart from the implementation

00:24:02.250 --> 00:24:03.720
inefficiencies.

00:24:03.720 --> 00:24:07.500
No matter what you do,
there is a fundamental limit

00:24:07.500 --> 00:24:09.090
to where you can
compress things.

00:24:09.090 --> 00:24:11.280
And it's very well
understood mathematically.

00:24:11.280 --> 00:24:16.230
And that's where the entropy
term gets introduced.

00:24:16.230 --> 00:24:18.390
And we'll explain it to you.

00:24:18.390 --> 00:24:20.880
So funny if somebody comes to
you with their billion dollar

00:24:20.880 --> 00:24:21.380
pitch.

00:24:21.380 --> 00:24:23.520
And they say, we can
reduce all the file sizes

00:24:23.520 --> 00:24:26.340
by, I don't know, 99.999999%.

00:24:26.340 --> 00:24:28.920
You can turn it
down very politely,

00:24:28.920 --> 00:24:31.470
after taking this course.

00:24:31.470 --> 00:24:32.640
Don't entertain.

00:24:36.150 --> 00:24:38.580
another fundamental
question is, for example,

00:24:38.580 --> 00:24:42.330
which we saw in the audios
and videos case, images case.

00:24:42.330 --> 00:24:45.750
Which is, how much
can I compress, if I'm

00:24:45.750 --> 00:24:48.320
OK to lose some information?

00:24:48.320 --> 00:24:50.040
Now, for example,
in that image case,

00:24:50.040 --> 00:24:53.510
we went from 515kb to 13kb.

00:24:53.510 --> 00:24:55.370
And we got some quality.

00:24:55.370 --> 00:24:57.830
Was this the best thing I
could have ever done could?

00:24:57.830 --> 00:24:59.690
I have done something better.

00:24:59.690 --> 00:25:03.560
And again there is a very
interesting, to the core,

00:25:03.560 --> 00:25:05.960
beautiful mathematical
theory for it,

00:25:05.960 --> 00:25:07.730
which is called rate
distortion theory.

00:25:07.730 --> 00:25:10.638
And you'll understand that there
are fundamental limitations

00:25:10.638 --> 00:25:11.930
to what you can do there again.

00:25:14.620 --> 00:25:17.170
So if you are into
theory, there is--

00:25:17.170 --> 00:25:19.420
there are some very
interesting questions, which

00:25:19.420 --> 00:25:22.650
involve compression.

00:25:22.650 --> 00:25:25.950
Maybe some of you
are from CS here.

00:25:25.950 --> 00:25:28.800
And you don't care
about theory as much.

00:25:28.800 --> 00:25:32.610
But you get very
excited with algorithms.

00:25:32.610 --> 00:25:33.765
You want to just--

00:25:36.280 --> 00:25:39.610
there are extremely
elegant algorithms which

00:25:39.610 --> 00:25:41.560
are involved in compression.

00:25:41.560 --> 00:25:44.840
And we'll introduce some
of them in the class.

00:25:44.840 --> 00:25:48.460
So here's a picture of Lempel
and Ziv which is the LZ.

00:25:48.460 --> 00:25:52.090
And hopefully, by the end
of this class you will be--

00:25:52.090 --> 00:25:52.780
you will know.

00:25:52.780 --> 00:25:54.970
So I'm throwing lots of
acronyms here at the end.

00:25:54.970 --> 00:25:56.798
But the hope is, by
the end of this class,

00:25:56.798 --> 00:25:58.090
you will be familiar with them.

00:25:58.090 --> 00:25:59.382
You'll be conversant with them.

00:25:59.382 --> 00:26:01.810
You'll be able to talk
with your peers, and others

00:26:01.810 --> 00:26:04.420
who work in
compression about them

00:26:04.420 --> 00:26:07.520
and be able to understand
these things technically.

00:26:07.520 --> 00:26:11.150
So for example, there is like
existence of universal lossless

00:26:11.150 --> 00:26:12.590
data compression algorithms.

00:26:12.590 --> 00:26:16.400
Which is the reason for
popularity of all these

00:26:16.400 --> 00:26:17.450
based schemes.

00:26:17.450 --> 00:26:20.605
For example, J Ziv is an
LZ based scheme scheme.

00:26:23.430 --> 00:26:25.800
Even more so, there
is all this usage

00:26:25.800 --> 00:26:30.750
of transforms in multimedia
compression like FFT, DCT.

00:26:30.750 --> 00:26:33.180
And you'll understand
why these algorithms

00:26:33.180 --> 00:26:35.640
and to be able to do these
things efficiently is extremely

00:26:35.640 --> 00:26:38.600
important for compression.

00:26:38.600 --> 00:26:41.225
And finally, very
interestingly, there

00:26:41.225 --> 00:26:43.520
are questions like how
are numeral systems?

00:26:43.520 --> 00:26:46.490
2 times 10 plus 2 equals to 22.

00:26:46.490 --> 00:26:49.160
How is this related
to compression?

00:26:49.160 --> 00:26:52.010
And you might find
it very surprising.

00:26:52.010 --> 00:26:54.440
But it is and you'll
learn in this class how.

00:26:57.620 --> 00:27:02.780
And finally, if you are someone
who is even a true engineer.

00:27:02.780 --> 00:27:05.780
Who works down goes right
down the assembly code

00:27:05.780 --> 00:27:07.640
or writes the most
efficient software code.

00:27:07.640 --> 00:27:09.390
You don't really care
about the algorithm.

00:27:09.390 --> 00:27:12.320
You want to optimize to the dot.

00:27:12.320 --> 00:27:14.900
There are some extremely
clever implementations

00:27:14.900 --> 00:27:16.940
involved in compression.

00:27:16.940 --> 00:27:18.680
Because as you
might imagine nobody

00:27:18.680 --> 00:27:20.960
has the time to
wait for your video

00:27:20.960 --> 00:27:23.570
file to decode in
a day, if you want

00:27:23.570 --> 00:27:27.200
to watch your favorite Netflix
stream or whatever it is.

00:27:27.200 --> 00:27:29.520
Just absolutely no one has time.

00:27:29.520 --> 00:27:32.300
And so there are some very
extreme clever implementations

00:27:32.300 --> 00:27:35.240
which people have
come through in this.

00:27:35.240 --> 00:27:38.480
So for example, I'm
going to just combine

00:27:38.480 --> 00:27:39.590
this in a single go.

00:27:39.590 --> 00:27:41.450
Which is, can we
cache computations?

00:27:41.450 --> 00:27:43.760
Very simple questions.

00:27:43.760 --> 00:27:47.060
But if you are someone who
has done computer architecture

00:27:47.060 --> 00:27:50.570
or hardware, you'll
be surprised to know

00:27:50.570 --> 00:27:55.400
there are specific hardware
implementations in your laptops

00:27:55.400 --> 00:27:58.920
right now, just for being
able to run a video decoder.

00:27:58.920 --> 00:28:01.500
Without which your videos
won't run real time

00:28:01.500 --> 00:28:03.710
on your favorite browser.

00:28:03.710 --> 00:28:05.900
So people have not only
optimized software,

00:28:05.900 --> 00:28:09.710
they have went on and worked
at the level of instruction set

00:28:09.710 --> 00:28:11.120
architectures.

00:28:11.120 --> 00:28:14.780
To optimize the compression
algorithms and theory

00:28:14.780 --> 00:28:17.310
which just preceded.

00:28:17.310 --> 00:28:19.297
This is a fun one.

00:28:19.297 --> 00:28:20.880
I don't know if you
guys have noticed,

00:28:20.880 --> 00:28:23.940
but if you are trying to go back
and forth on YouTube, Netflix,

00:28:23.940 --> 00:28:28.170
Prime, whatever it is, there
is some smallest delta at which

00:28:28.170 --> 00:28:29.400
you can go back and forth.

00:28:29.400 --> 00:28:30.480
Maybe it's 5 seconds.

00:28:30.480 --> 00:28:34.240
Maybe it's 1 second,
2 second, depends.

00:28:34.240 --> 00:28:37.570
But notice, try it
after you go home today.

00:28:37.570 --> 00:28:39.970
YouTube, you may be,
will have to check.

00:28:39.970 --> 00:28:41.825
I think it's 5, no it's 1.

00:28:41.825 --> 00:28:42.700
I think I don't know.

00:28:42.700 --> 00:28:46.270
But at some point
it used to be 5.

00:28:46.270 --> 00:28:49.660
And if you go back
and forth, you

00:28:49.660 --> 00:28:53.830
can't go back and forth in
units smaller than them.

00:28:53.830 --> 00:28:55.270
And that's also
very interesting.

00:28:55.270 --> 00:28:57.640
Because it comes around
from a clever implementation

00:28:57.640 --> 00:29:01.040
for you being able to
stream videos real time.

00:29:01.040 --> 00:29:03.830
If you don't do that, if you
try to give a millisecond level

00:29:03.830 --> 00:29:07.880
stream, millisecond level
seek for the videos,

00:29:07.880 --> 00:29:10.310
you won't be able to
decompress at real time.

00:29:10.310 --> 00:29:12.440
And then you'll start seeing
the annoying bar which

00:29:12.440 --> 00:29:16.740
is the annoying circle
which everyone hates.

00:29:16.740 --> 00:29:19.200
So that's actually
a side phenomena

00:29:19.200 --> 00:29:20.610
which is coming from the--

00:29:20.610 --> 00:29:22.680
from a clever implementation.

00:29:22.680 --> 00:29:26.550
And there basically, you have
something called frame groups,

00:29:26.550 --> 00:29:27.570
I/P/B frames.

00:29:27.570 --> 00:29:29.320
Which is again something
which we'll touch

00:29:29.320 --> 00:29:33.060
in this lecture at some point.

00:29:33.060 --> 00:29:35.560
And finally, there are also
some interesting data structures

00:29:35.560 --> 00:29:38.583
which shows up like suffix
trees and BWT transform.

00:29:38.583 --> 00:29:40.000
In fact, they were
data structures

00:29:40.000 --> 00:29:42.070
which were designed
for compression

00:29:42.070 --> 00:29:45.055
and now are mainstream in
certain places like genomics.

00:29:49.180 --> 00:29:52.570
And so really the takeaway
of all of these examples,

00:29:52.570 --> 00:29:56.680
is that no matter what you like
there is something fun for you

00:29:56.680 --> 00:29:57.940
in the field of compression.

00:29:57.940 --> 00:30:00.100
And in this class, how
this class I would say

00:30:00.100 --> 00:30:03.640
differs from the other
offerings, even at Stanford.

00:30:03.640 --> 00:30:05.230
Is that we'll try
to give you a flavor

00:30:05.230 --> 00:30:10.870
of all these three and packed
in a quarters framework.

00:30:14.330 --> 00:30:16.550
So in particular, the
first half of the course.

00:30:16.550 --> 00:30:18.480
So now we'll get a little
bit into the course.

00:30:18.480 --> 00:30:19.855
The first half of
the course will

00:30:19.855 --> 00:30:21.620
deal with lossless compression.

00:30:21.620 --> 00:30:24.770
Which is if you don't
lose any information.

00:30:24.770 --> 00:30:27.350
And the second half would deal
with the lossy compression.

00:30:27.350 --> 00:30:29.570
Which is I'm OK to
lose some information.

00:30:29.570 --> 00:30:32.610
What's the best I can do now?

00:30:32.610 --> 00:30:34.230
And throughout the
course, we'll keep

00:30:34.230 --> 00:30:37.110
showing you workings of various
tools and code snippets.

00:30:37.110 --> 00:30:38.820
So there'll be a mix up.

00:30:38.820 --> 00:30:40.890
We'll also show you some
theory, some proofs.

00:30:40.890 --> 00:30:43.090
But we'll also show you
like lots of tools and code

00:30:43.090 --> 00:30:43.590
snippets.

00:30:43.590 --> 00:30:45.495
So hopefully, it will
be fun for everyone.

00:30:45.495 --> 00:30:47.370
And we'll try to appreciate
the same material

00:30:47.370 --> 00:30:48.330
from different angles.

00:30:51.450 --> 00:30:53.040
So this is just course outline.

00:30:53.040 --> 00:30:54.720
It's also available
on the website.

00:30:54.720 --> 00:30:56.970
But let me just go
through real quick.

00:30:56.970 --> 00:30:58.920
So on lossless
compression side, we'll

00:30:58.920 --> 00:31:02.543
talk about entropy and its
fundamental role in compression

00:31:02.543 --> 00:31:03.585
like we have been saying.

00:31:06.270 --> 00:31:07.680
I think till week 2, roughly.

00:31:07.680 --> 00:31:10.500
And then we'll start getting
into the lossless compressors

00:31:10.500 --> 00:31:13.110
right from week 3 of the class.

00:31:13.110 --> 00:31:15.690
And we will study
lots of compressors

00:31:15.690 --> 00:31:19.110
like Huffman, arithmetic coding,
asymmetric numeral systems,

00:31:19.110 --> 00:31:20.190
your LZ.

00:31:20.190 --> 00:31:22.170
And hopefully, by
the end of it all

00:31:22.170 --> 00:31:23.730
you would have a
deeper understanding

00:31:23.730 --> 00:31:24.750
of how gzip works.

00:31:24.750 --> 00:31:26.640
Or how you can
improve, do lossless

00:31:26.640 --> 00:31:29.550
compression much better for
whatever application you have.

00:31:29.550 --> 00:31:32.320
We'll also talk about how you
can handle correlated sources.

00:31:32.320 --> 00:31:35.960
So things like adaptive
arithmetic coding.

00:31:35.960 --> 00:31:37.970
And this is going to be
roughly till lecture 10.

00:31:37.970 --> 00:31:40.340
So first half of the class.

00:31:40.340 --> 00:31:42.080
And then the second
half of the class

00:31:42.080 --> 00:31:43.700
will be around
lossy compression.

00:31:43.700 --> 00:31:46.790
Where I said, I've been
saying for some time,

00:31:46.790 --> 00:31:49.970
we'll talk about rate distortion
theory and mutual information.

00:31:49.970 --> 00:31:51.530
We'll talk about quantization.

00:31:51.530 --> 00:31:53.150
We'll talk about
transform coding.

00:31:53.150 --> 00:31:55.340
All these different transforms
why do they show up?

00:31:55.340 --> 00:31:56.750
Why do they matter?

00:31:56.750 --> 00:31:59.630
And then we'll have basically
a very deep dive into images.

00:31:59.630 --> 00:32:02.810
I think roughly 2 and
1/2, 3 lectures almost.

00:32:02.810 --> 00:32:04.670
Where we'll talk about
image compression.

00:32:04.670 --> 00:32:07.887
Because a lot of these
principles tie together there.

00:32:07.887 --> 00:32:09.470
In particular, we'll
talk a little bit

00:32:09.470 --> 00:32:15.110
about how JPEG works, BPG works,
as well as how ML in particular

00:32:15.110 --> 00:32:16.760
has been used to
improve compressors.

00:32:16.760 --> 00:32:18.087
So the new age compressors.

00:32:18.087 --> 00:32:19.670
People have been
trying to incorporate

00:32:19.670 --> 00:32:20.780
all these new findings.

00:32:20.780 --> 00:32:22.850
So we'll have a dedicated
lecture on that.

00:32:22.850 --> 00:32:26.200
On how you can do
learned compression.

00:32:26.200 --> 00:32:30.100
And finally, we'll wrap it
up with video compression.

00:32:30.100 --> 00:32:32.140
Because as I think
I've been highlighting,

00:32:32.140 --> 00:32:35.050
video is really big part
of your streaming pipeline.

00:32:35.050 --> 00:32:38.020
And some role of human
perception and lossy

00:32:38.020 --> 00:32:38.800
compression.

00:32:38.800 --> 00:32:40.900
And the hope is by
the end of this.

00:32:40.900 --> 00:32:42.580
What I'm showing
