00:36:16.360 --> 00:36:18.250
as we start with the theory.

00:36:18.250 --> 00:36:21.070
And you might have done
other theory courses

00:36:21.070 --> 00:36:23.920
or seen theory in other classes.

00:36:23.920 --> 00:36:27.610
One thing I find very nice
about information theory

00:36:27.610 --> 00:36:31.090
and compression is, the
theory is really useful.

00:36:31.090 --> 00:36:33.100
It's not some abstract thing.

00:36:33.100 --> 00:36:35.620
It applies to some very
specific simpler settings.

00:36:35.620 --> 00:36:38.800
But those simpler settings are
very prevalent in real life.

00:36:38.800 --> 00:36:42.310
Even this Monday I was--

00:36:42.310 --> 00:36:43.240
something in my work.

00:36:43.240 --> 00:36:45.490
And I thought, can
I compress this

00:36:45.490 --> 00:36:49.450
better than this gzip or
zStandard is compressing it.

00:36:49.450 --> 00:36:52.750
And I just wrote a
short Python notebook.

00:36:52.750 --> 00:36:54.100
Was able to determine, no.

00:36:54.100 --> 00:36:57.130
There is a gap with just
