00:37:18.820 --> 00:37:21.910
So there was a question
before about what probability

00:37:21.910 --> 00:37:24.880
background do we expect.

00:37:24.880 --> 00:37:30.730
So you should be familiar with
probability random variables,

00:37:30.730 --> 00:37:32.650
expectation.

00:37:32.650 --> 00:37:35.170
Conditional probability
will come in a bit later.

00:37:35.170 --> 00:37:38.840
Joint distribution
of two variables.

00:37:38.840 --> 00:37:39.850
That's really it.

00:37:39.850 --> 00:37:41.142
Expectation will come in a lot.

00:37:41.142 --> 00:37:42.975
So you should know what
expectation is, what

00:37:42.975 --> 00:37:44.320
is a conditional expectation?

00:37:44.320 --> 00:37:46.090
That thing.

00:37:46.090 --> 00:37:51.910
You don't need to know of
advanced probability like Borel

00:37:51.910 --> 00:37:52.450
spaces.

00:37:52.450 --> 00:37:54.220
That thing.

00:37:54.220 --> 00:37:56.110
That's just basic first
course in probability

00:37:56.110 --> 00:37:58.270
is what you need.

00:37:58.270 --> 00:38:03.190
So let's get started
with very simple setting.

00:38:03.190 --> 00:38:07.820
You have a alphabet.

00:38:07.820 --> 00:38:11.450
So every probability space,
you usually have an alphabet.

00:38:11.450 --> 00:38:13.310
So here the alphabet
is just ABCD.

00:38:13.310 --> 00:38:15.440
You have just four
symbols in your alphabet.

00:38:15.440 --> 00:38:17.480
And we have a very
simple distribution.

00:38:17.480 --> 00:38:20.390
We have all of them
are equally likely.

00:38:20.390 --> 00:38:24.050
So each of them has a
probability of one fourth.

00:38:24.050 --> 00:38:26.870
So what I do is, I
create a text file.

00:38:26.870 --> 00:38:29.780
I just independently
sample among these

00:38:29.780 --> 00:38:31.550
with this uniform distribution.

00:38:31.550 --> 00:38:33.440
And I get a file.

00:38:33.440 --> 00:38:35.540
I sample 1 million symbols.

00:38:35.540 --> 00:38:39.200
So as you know from the
law of large numbers, what

00:38:39.200 --> 00:38:40.910
I would see is, in
the file I would

00:38:40.910 --> 00:38:49.210
have around 250,000 A's 50,000
B's, 250,000 C's and so on.

00:38:49.210 --> 00:38:51.990
So can somebody
tell me what will be

00:38:51.990 --> 00:38:54.525
the size of this file on disk?

00:38:58.240 --> 00:39:01.510
It depends what you choose
to represent the sample,

00:39:01.510 --> 00:39:04.180
as in terms of bits.

00:39:04.180 --> 00:39:10.930
If you use one byte per symbol
then it'd be 8 million bits.

00:39:10.930 --> 00:39:13.750
But if you use 2
bits, because there's

00:39:13.750 --> 00:39:15.970
only four
possibilities, then that

00:39:15.970 --> 00:39:19.960
can make it 2 million bits.

00:39:19.960 --> 00:39:20.680
Yeah.

00:39:20.680 --> 00:39:25.480
So the answer I got was,
it depends on how many bits

00:39:25.480 --> 00:39:27.850
you use per symbol.

00:39:27.850 --> 00:39:30.880
And a typical file as
we will see shortly.

00:39:30.880 --> 00:39:32.770
A typical way, if
you write in Python

00:39:32.770 --> 00:39:35.170
and just create this file.

00:39:35.170 --> 00:39:39.100
You would use 8 million
bits or 1 million bytes.

00:39:39.100 --> 00:39:40.750
And we will see why shortly.

00:39:40.750 --> 00:39:43.570
Thank you.

00:39:43.570 --> 00:39:46.810
So before we start about it,
Pulkit talked about a lot

00:39:46.810 --> 00:39:51.460
about going up to even
exabytes and zettabytes.

00:39:51.460 --> 00:39:55.510
But what is a bit
what is a byte.

00:39:55.510 --> 00:39:57.460
So this is just
from the dictionary.

00:39:57.460 --> 00:40:00.310
So a bit is just a
unit of information

00:40:00.310 --> 00:40:02.740
expressed as either a 0 or a 1.

00:40:02.740 --> 00:40:06.070
For us, the way to think about
bit is, a bit is either 0 or 1.

00:40:06.070 --> 00:40:07.930
So it is two possibilities.

00:40:07.930 --> 00:40:09.400
Every bit is a 0 or a 1.

00:40:09.400 --> 00:40:13.950
And those who are
familiar with computers,

00:40:13.950 --> 00:40:17.310
you know bit is the fundamental
unit at which things

00:40:17.310 --> 00:40:22.040
works with semiconductors
and transistors and so on.

00:40:22.040 --> 00:40:24.080
An underbite is a
group of 8 bits.

00:40:24.080 --> 00:40:27.590
So 256 values.

00:40:27.590 --> 00:40:29.000
Each bit is 2 values.

00:40:29.000 --> 00:40:32.750
A byte can take 256
possible values to power it.

00:40:32.750 --> 00:40:39.000
So 1 byte is 8
bits and then what

00:40:39.000 --> 00:40:45.300
1 kilobyte is 1,000 bytes
which is 8,000 bits and so on.

00:40:45.300 --> 00:40:49.260
Just a warning, that
sometimes people

00:40:49.260 --> 00:40:52.800
use powers of 2 instead
of powers of 10.

00:40:52.800 --> 00:40:59.940
So usually it's very close,
but in my work sometimes.

00:40:59.940 --> 00:41:03.440
So you have 1 exabyte,
if you represent it

00:41:03.440 --> 00:41:06.260
as 10 power 18,
that's one number.

00:41:06.260 --> 00:41:09.620
But when you represent
it as 2 power 60,

00:41:09.620 --> 00:41:12.320
it actually starts to
matter at very large values.

00:41:12.320 --> 00:41:15.620
Whether you use powers
of 10 or powers of 2.

00:41:15.620 --> 00:41:18.320
So just a warning, when you're
dealing with big numbers,

00:41:18.320 --> 00:41:19.040
be careful.

00:41:19.040 --> 00:41:20.600
Whether someone is talking.

00:41:20.600 --> 00:41:23.420
When they say kilobyte,
do they mean 1024 bytes

00:41:23.420 --> 00:41:30.560
or do they mean 1,000 bytes?

00:41:30.560 --> 00:41:33.050
So if you actually create
this file, you check the size.

00:41:33.050 --> 00:41:37.160
What you would see is the size
is 1 megabyte, 1 million bytes.

00:41:37.160 --> 00:41:41.150
And we are basically spending
1 byte per each symbol.

00:41:41.150 --> 00:41:41.650
ABCD.

00:41:45.160 --> 00:41:49.390
So many of you, I assume,
are familiar with ASCII.

00:41:49.390 --> 00:41:50.500
ASCII is just an encoding.

00:41:50.500 --> 00:41:53.500
So when you need to represent
anything in terms of bits,

00:41:53.500 --> 00:41:56.290
you need a way to encode it.

00:41:56.290 --> 00:41:58.870
So this is like a
standard way to encode it.

00:41:58.870 --> 00:42:03.680
You can see a few of your
favorite things here.

00:42:03.680 --> 00:42:07.670
Here you see A
through A, uppercase.

00:42:07.670 --> 00:42:10.670
Here you see a
through z, lowercase.

00:42:10.670 --> 00:42:14.410
Here is your numbers,
punctuations.

00:42:17.500 --> 00:42:22.030
And for each of those like
A, A is represented as a 65.

00:42:22.030 --> 00:42:24.730
And then B is represented as 66.

00:42:24.730 --> 00:42:27.190
So it's just a way
of representing

00:42:27.190 --> 00:42:29.410
all of these things
we see on our keyboard

00:42:29.410 --> 00:42:32.050
as numbers, and hence as bits.

00:42:35.910 --> 00:42:39.060
So this is the ASCII
table, for example,

00:42:39.060 --> 00:42:42.180
for ABCD, our alphabet.

00:42:42.180 --> 00:42:45.630
And you see we are using 8
bits for each of the values.

00:42:49.560 --> 00:42:52.510
Any questions?

00:42:52.510 --> 00:42:55.510
This is very simple,
as Pulkit said.

00:42:55.510 --> 00:43:00.880
It will get progressively more
interesting and complicated.

00:43:00.880 --> 00:43:04.210
So actually someone already
answered this question.

00:43:04.210 --> 00:43:05.200
Can we do better?

00:43:05.200 --> 00:43:06.730
And what they said was, yes.

00:43:06.730 --> 00:43:07.600
We can do better.

00:43:07.600 --> 00:43:08.140
Why?

00:43:08.140 --> 00:43:10.630
Because if you see here,
this doesn't seem like.

00:43:10.630 --> 00:43:12.970
If you know that your
file only has ABCD

00:43:12.970 --> 00:43:15.940
this doesn't seem like
the best use of your bits.

00:43:15.940 --> 00:43:21.070
See this part is the
same for all four, 10000.

00:43:21.070 --> 00:43:23.950
So you just seem to be
wasting a bunch of bits

00:43:23.950 --> 00:43:26.920
for each of those symbols.

00:43:26.920 --> 00:43:33.370
To explain what I mean,
let's see this code.

00:43:33.370 --> 00:43:37.990
So what we are doing
now is, we just use one,

00:43:37.990 --> 00:43:39.655
sorry, two bits per symbol.

00:43:43.820 --> 00:43:47.150
Two bits for every symbol.

00:43:47.150 --> 00:43:49.490
So if you have the
same file of one

00:43:49.490 --> 00:43:52.010
megabyte of 1 million symbols.

00:43:52.010 --> 00:43:58.940
Now, it would use 2 million
bits which is 250 kb.

00:43:58.940 --> 00:44:04.745
So now, it's 250 kb instead
of the 1 mb were using before.

00:44:08.620 --> 00:44:13.820
Someone suggests, if I
use this code are there

00:44:13.820 --> 00:44:14.990
any considerations?

00:44:14.990 --> 00:44:18.380
If I create a file using this
code, I use 2 bits per symbol.

00:44:18.380 --> 00:44:21.260
And then I send this file
to anybody, somebody.

00:44:21.260 --> 00:44:23.510
What do they need to in
order to read the file?

00:44:26.130 --> 00:44:30.180
They need to know what
symbol represents what bits.

00:44:30.180 --> 00:44:33.720
So if they think that
0 is equal to d then

00:44:33.720 --> 00:44:35.430
they won't be reading it.

00:44:35.430 --> 00:44:40.380
So you have to set
the key [INAUDIBLE]

00:44:42.240 --> 00:44:42.960
The others agree.

00:44:42.960 --> 00:44:44.280
Any other suggestions?

00:44:48.160 --> 00:44:48.880
Yeah.

00:44:48.880 --> 00:44:52.570
So the so the answer was, they
need to know how to decode it.

00:44:52.570 --> 00:44:55.360
Basically, they need to know
this table in simple words?

00:44:55.360 --> 00:44:58.450
If they have this table,
they can decode the file.

00:44:58.450 --> 00:45:01.480
They receive this
0001 and so on.

00:45:01.480 --> 00:45:05.800
But unless they know that
my four symbols are ABCD.

00:45:05.800 --> 00:45:08.560
How do they know it was not
EFGH or one two three four

00:45:08.560 --> 00:45:09.648
or something?

00:45:09.648 --> 00:45:11.440
Just because they get
the file, they can't.

00:45:11.440 --> 00:45:12.760
That's why we use ASCII.

00:45:12.760 --> 00:45:14.200
That's the whole point of ASCII.

00:45:14.200 --> 00:45:16.180
That Everybody
knows what ASCII is.

00:45:16.180 --> 00:45:17.830
It's a standard thing.

00:45:17.830 --> 00:45:21.220
So when you send a file they
know it's an ASCII file.

00:45:21.220 --> 00:45:23.530
They can read it.

00:45:23.530 --> 00:45:26.140
And we will see a lot of
compressors in the class.

00:45:26.140 --> 00:45:28.030
And every time you
use a compressor,

00:45:28.030 --> 00:45:32.550
you need to indicate to
the receiver basically,

00:45:32.550 --> 00:45:34.440
what compressor did you use.

00:45:34.440 --> 00:45:36.450
You might have seen
file extensions.

00:45:36.450 --> 00:45:38.220
Sometimes they play that role.

00:45:38.220 --> 00:45:40.275
You have a dot PDF or
dot zip or whatever.

00:45:45.660 --> 00:45:49.420
So I guess this should
have been x which is what

00:45:49.420 --> 00:45:51.700
we're using for the alphabet.

00:45:51.700 --> 00:45:55.840
So if you have k
symbols, then you

00:45:55.840 --> 00:46:02.490
use log k bits in a
fixed bit width code.

00:46:02.490 --> 00:46:05.280
Let me explain.

00:46:05.280 --> 00:46:13.690
So if we have two
symbols, you just

00:46:13.690 --> 00:46:15.790
need one bit right
because two symbols

00:46:15.790 --> 00:46:18.220
means you have just 0 or 1.

00:46:18.220 --> 00:46:20.530
If you have four
symbols we already saw.

00:46:20.530 --> 00:46:23.814
For four symbols you
have 00/ 01 /10/ 11.

00:46:26.440 --> 00:46:32.230
If you have eight symbols then
you could do 000/ 001/ 010,

00:46:32.230 --> 00:46:35.440
so on.

00:46:35.440 --> 00:46:39.990
What would I do if I had
three symbols, let's say?

00:46:42.840 --> 00:46:43.950
How many bits do I need?

00:46:48.040 --> 00:46:49.500
Two bits

00:46:49.500 --> 00:46:51.990
Two bits.

00:46:51.990 --> 00:46:54.030
What are the two bits?

00:46:54.030 --> 00:46:57.930
How-- let's say it's just 1, 2,
3 is what I'm trying to encode.

00:46:57.930 --> 00:47:00.710
Can you suggest an encoding?

00:47:00.710 --> 00:47:03.980
000.

00:47:03.980 --> 00:47:05.660
Yeah.

00:47:05.660 --> 00:47:07.570
So that's one way you could.

00:47:07.570 --> 00:47:09.985
A bunch of possible things,
doesn't matter really

00:47:09.985 --> 00:47:10.610
like you could.

00:47:13.740 --> 00:47:17.940
Any of the possible
combinations here?

00:47:17.940 --> 00:47:19.610
So is this clear to everyone?

00:47:19.610 --> 00:47:20.460
Good

00:47:20.460 --> 00:47:22.110
The last line [INAUDIBLE]

00:47:22.110 --> 00:47:23.190
Oh, I see.

00:47:30.650 --> 00:47:32.630
Let me rewrite it here.

00:47:32.630 --> 00:47:33.845
1, 2, 3.

00:47:36.390 --> 00:47:38.610
00/ 01/ 10.

00:47:38.610 --> 00:47:42.150
Or you could do another
encoding which is this thing.

00:47:42.150 --> 00:47:43.650
Lots of possibilities there.

00:47:47.120 --> 00:47:49.520
For the three simple
case, do we think

00:47:49.520 --> 00:47:52.640
this is like the best
you can do or do you

00:47:52.640 --> 00:47:55.940
think we should be
able to do better?

00:47:55.940 --> 00:47:58.400
I guess the person who answered.

00:47:58.400 --> 00:48:00.080
Go ahead.

00:48:00.080 --> 00:48:02.000
In this case, we should
be able to do better.

00:48:02.000 --> 00:48:03.740
Because for instance,
we could have

00:48:03.740 --> 00:48:06.530
one of them just start
with one and the other two

00:48:06.530 --> 00:48:07.250
start with zero.

00:48:07.250 --> 00:48:09.800
So you could have one of the
symbols encoded with only one

00:48:09.800 --> 00:48:12.630
bit instead of two.

00:48:12.630 --> 00:48:13.130
Yeah.

00:48:13.130 --> 00:48:15.230
So the suggestion
is that who is--

00:48:15.230 --> 00:48:17.942
nobody is forcing us to always
use two bits per symbol.

00:48:17.942 --> 00:48:19.400
Maybe there are
other schemes where

00:48:19.400 --> 00:48:22.520
we use different number of
bits for different symbols.

00:48:22.520 --> 00:48:24.650
That's a good idea.

00:48:24.650 --> 00:48:28.520
There is another idea which you
will actually see in the quiz

00:48:28.520 --> 00:48:29.390
today.

00:48:29.390 --> 00:48:32.900
That's a slightly
different idea ultimately.

00:48:32.900 --> 00:48:35.810
It's a variation
of the same thing.

00:48:35.810 --> 00:48:38.870
But yes, as you can see, for
two symbols this seems OK.

00:48:38.870 --> 00:48:40.250
This is exactly right.

00:48:40.250 --> 00:48:43.100
You exhaust the whole
code space in a way.

00:48:43.100 --> 00:48:44.990
For four symbols
this looks exactly.

00:48:44.990 --> 00:48:49.460
For three already you start
to see that it's not trivial.

00:48:49.460 --> 00:48:51.530
You need to do some work too.

00:48:51.530 --> 00:48:53.780
Because why are we using
the same number of bits

00:48:53.780 --> 00:48:55.800
for three symbols
and for four symbols?

00:48:55.800 --> 00:48:58.560
So clearly there is
some inefficiency.

00:48:58.560 --> 00:49:00.570
So what you will
see very soon is,

00:49:00.570 --> 00:49:04.290
this fixed bitwidth code is
not a particularly good code

00:49:04.290 --> 00:49:05.520
in most scenarios.

00:49:05.520 --> 00:49:07.350
If you want to use the
same number of bits

00:49:07.350 --> 00:49:12.120
for every symbol, very soon
you run into all limitations.

00:49:12.120 --> 00:49:15.195
And we will see in a
minute, something better.

00:49:19.650 --> 00:49:23.280
If we don't fix this for
each of these letters,

00:49:23.280 --> 00:49:26.050
how can you decode it?

00:49:26.050 --> 00:49:28.230
The length varies for each code.

00:49:29.310 --> 00:49:32.220
So the question is like if
we don't fix the bitwidth,

00:49:32.220 --> 00:49:35.620
how will we even decode it?

00:49:35.620 --> 00:49:37.170
We'll see a code later today.

00:49:37.170 --> 00:49:39.630
And I will ask you to
actually decode it at home

00:49:39.630 --> 00:49:41.385
and come back to me
in the next lecture.

00:49:43.890 --> 00:49:44.670
There are ways.

00:49:44.670 --> 00:49:46.290
We'll figure it out together.

00:49:46.290 --> 00:49:47.190
Yes.

00:49:47.190 --> 00:49:48.802
But that's a valid point.

00:49:48.802 --> 00:49:50.760
One thing to like about
the fixed bitwidth code

00:49:50.760 --> 00:49:52.620
is, it's like trivial to decode.

00:49:52.620 --> 00:49:56.880
I give you a file
with, let's say

00:49:56.880 --> 00:50:04.150
you're using three
bits per symbol code.

00:50:04.150 --> 00:50:06.940
And then I give you a file of--

00:50:06.940 --> 00:50:11.500
I give you some file
like 000101110, whatever.

00:50:11.500 --> 00:50:16.780
You just divide it into
sections of like three

00:50:16.780 --> 00:50:18.490
and then you can
decode each of those.

00:50:18.490 --> 00:50:21.370
It's very simple to decode it.

00:50:21.370 --> 00:50:23.680
But the more complicated
codes will be more

00:50:23.680 --> 00:50:24.970
complicated to decode also.

00:50:29.450 --> 00:50:31.190
So with this we already saw it.

00:50:31.190 --> 00:50:33.950
If you have a uniform
distribution, each of these

00:50:33.950 --> 00:50:36.800
is equally likely you
spend one bit per symbol,

00:50:36.800 --> 00:50:38.960
this seems good,
this seems optimal.

00:50:38.960 --> 00:50:42.350
We don't think this is like
there is any issue here.

00:50:42.350 --> 00:50:44.330
Obviously, if A and
B are equally likely,

00:50:44.330 --> 00:50:46.130
you should be--
need to-- you should

00:50:46.130 --> 00:50:47.540
need to use one bit per symbol.

00:50:50.330 --> 00:50:55.220
Now, see I slightly
modified the distribution.

00:50:55.220 --> 00:50:58.970
Here you had just and b
and both were like 50/50.

00:50:58.970 --> 00:51:04.230
Now I said no I
have C and D also,

00:51:04.230 --> 00:51:07.350
but they have lower probability.

00:51:07.350 --> 00:51:09.990
So what I'm saying is
it's almost always A or B

00:51:09.990 --> 00:51:12.960
but in very rare cases
it can be C or D.

00:51:12.960 --> 00:51:15.210
This looks like a very similar
distribution to the one

00:51:15.210 --> 00:51:18.350
we already had in
the last slide.

00:51:18.350 --> 00:51:21.230
But now suddenly you see that
if you use your fixed bitwidth

00:51:21.230 --> 00:51:26.060
code, you start to use
two bits per symbol.

00:51:26.060 --> 00:51:29.690
So you already should start
seeing some inefficiency like,

00:51:29.690 --> 00:51:33.410
no clearly this is not the best
thing you should be able to do.

00:51:33.410 --> 00:51:35.510
This is so close
to the previous one

00:51:35.510 --> 00:51:41.060
we want to be able to use
basically one bit per symbol.

00:51:41.060 --> 00:51:43.750
Any questions so far?

00:51:43.750 --> 00:51:48.320
See this is my genomics
background coming in.

00:51:48.320 --> 00:51:54.920
In genomics you have base ACGT
and accidentally rotate anyway.

00:51:59.690 --> 00:52:02.660
So one solution which
was hinted by Pulkit,

00:52:02.660 --> 00:52:05.990
but which we won't
really do now, is--

00:52:05.990 --> 00:52:09.380
I don't care about C and
D. If I see a C or a D,

00:52:09.380 --> 00:52:11.540
I will just encode
it as A or B .

00:52:11.540 --> 00:52:17.000
It will only happen 2%
of the time, who cares?

00:52:17.000 --> 00:52:20.000
This would actually be fine
in some of the image and video

00:52:20.000 --> 00:52:22.640
compression parts, because
they are the-- what

00:52:22.640 --> 00:52:24.080
matters is human perception.

00:52:24.080 --> 00:52:25.430
You don't want to--

00:52:25.430 --> 00:52:29.600
you are fine with if one
pixel changes for example.

00:52:29.600 --> 00:52:31.490
And we'll see much
more nuance there.

00:52:31.490 --> 00:52:37.220
But usually for text, or logs,
or databases, or PDF files,

00:52:37.220 --> 00:52:39.680
or something, you don't
want to lose any bit at all,

00:52:39.680 --> 00:52:42.140
it has to be lossless.

00:52:42.140 --> 00:52:45.020
That's the traditional
way people do it,

00:52:45.020 --> 00:52:47.390
maybe things will change.

00:52:47.390 --> 00:52:49.670
But lossless
compression is important

00:52:49.670 --> 00:52:52.375
not only because the
application demands it, but also

00:52:52.375 --> 00:52:53.750
when you do lossy
compression you

00:52:53.750 --> 00:52:56.090
will see that the lossy
compression is often built

00:52:56.090 --> 00:52:58.110
on top of lossless compressors.

00:52:58.110 --> 00:53:00.270
So the first half of the
course will basically

00:53:00.270 --> 00:53:03.060
provide you the foundation for
the second half of the course.

00:53:03.060 --> 00:53:05.400
So we do want to
losslessly compress it,

00:53:05.400 --> 00:53:09.445
we don't want to lose the
C's and the D's So actually

00:53:09.445 --> 00:53:11.070
like some people have
already suggested

00:53:11.070 --> 00:53:13.920
a solution which is to use
variable length codes which

00:53:13.920 --> 00:53:16.540
we will now see.

00:53:16.540 --> 00:53:17.860
Variable length codes.

00:53:17.860 --> 00:53:21.160
This is like the key idea
for today's lecture really.

00:53:21.160 --> 00:53:26.890
This should be a takeaway, and
let's see it here actually.

00:53:26.890 --> 00:53:31.810
So simple idea, you
have things which

00:53:31.810 --> 00:53:35.260
are more probable you have
things which are less probable.

00:53:35.260 --> 00:53:38.830
Let's use less number of bits
for the more probable stuff

00:53:38.830 --> 00:53:41.620
and more number of bits for
the less probable stuff.

00:53:41.620 --> 00:53:44.860
That's obvious, you want to
reduce your overall expenditure

00:53:44.860 --> 00:53:47.350
of bits, you want to make your
file as small as possible.

00:53:47.350 --> 00:53:49.210
So let's do it.

00:53:49.210 --> 00:53:50.920
And we'll come back
to the question

00:53:50.920 --> 00:53:53.690
that was asked about how
will you even decode it.

00:53:53.690 --> 00:53:55.450
Let's come back to that later.

00:53:55.450 --> 00:53:59.770
For now trust me that
this can be decoded.

00:53:59.770 --> 00:54:01.612
So do we understand
the code right so

00:54:01.612 --> 00:54:03.820
what are we saying is that
A will be decoded as zero,

00:54:03.820 --> 00:54:06.400
B will be decoded as
one-- encoded as one zero.

00:54:06.400 --> 00:54:09.130
C is as 110, and D as 111.

00:54:09.130 --> 00:54:14.020
So let's say you had
A, C, D as your thing.

00:54:14.020 --> 00:54:18.010
After you encode it, what you
will get is, A will become a 0,

00:54:18.010 --> 00:54:22.770
C will become a 110,
D will become a 111.

00:54:22.770 --> 00:54:25.620
So hopefully that part is clear.

00:54:25.620 --> 00:54:29.430
The code just is a way to have
a sequence of input symbols,

00:54:29.430 --> 00:54:31.920
you are able to encode
it as a sequence of bits.

00:54:31.920 --> 00:54:33.045
And why do we use bits?

00:54:33.045 --> 00:54:34.170
We already talked about it.

00:54:34.170 --> 00:54:37.050
Because bits is the unit
of storage on a computer,

00:54:37.050 --> 00:54:39.750
so you need to ultimately
convert everything to bits.

00:54:42.440 --> 00:54:43.550
Any questions so far?

00:54:47.640 --> 00:54:48.690
So remember this idea?

00:54:48.690 --> 00:54:51.870
Really this is
takeaway for today.

00:54:51.870 --> 00:54:56.200
Fewer bits for more viable
symbols very obvious.

00:54:56.200 --> 00:55:01.140
Now, see the last one we
had, the previous code

00:55:01.140 --> 00:55:03.840
we were talking about
was two bits per symbol.

00:55:03.840 --> 00:55:05.728
That was very
simple to evaluate,

00:55:05.728 --> 00:55:07.770
you would just-- it is
using two bits per symbol.

00:55:07.770 --> 00:55:11.040
Here it's trickier, here it's--
for some symbols you're using

00:55:11.040 --> 00:55:13.950
just one bit, for some symbols
you're using three bits.

00:55:13.950 --> 00:55:17.250
So is it better than
the previous one?

00:55:17.250 --> 00:55:19.740
Can somebody suggest
how would you--

00:55:19.740 --> 00:55:22.290
first of all, do you think
it is better than just using

00:55:22.290 --> 00:55:25.720
a two bits per symbol code?

00:55:25.720 --> 00:55:29.200
It should be better since
roughly half the time we're

00:55:29.200 --> 00:55:31.900
using one bit instead of two.

00:55:31.900 --> 00:55:33.730
And then to evaluate
it, you can just

00:55:33.730 --> 00:55:36.160
look at the bits per symbol
which is just multiplying

00:55:36.160 --> 00:55:38.300
the probability by the
length for each of them

00:55:38.300 --> 00:55:40.510
and adding them together.

00:55:40.510 --> 00:55:43.030
So the-- and servers
which is correct

00:55:43.030 --> 00:55:48.670
is, you can say that half of the
time you're using just one bit.

00:55:48.670 --> 00:55:50.680
So in this case you're
just using one bit

00:55:50.680 --> 00:55:53.440
and here you're using two bits.

00:55:53.440 --> 00:55:57.820
So on average you are roughly
using 1.5 bits per symbol.

00:55:57.820 --> 00:56:00.640
The other two we will
ignore for the moment

00:56:00.640 --> 00:56:02.890
because they are very
low probability events.

00:56:02.890 --> 00:56:05.740
So basically you're
using 1.5 bits per symbol

00:56:05.740 --> 00:56:09.190
instead of using
two bits per symbol.

00:56:09.190 --> 00:56:10.750
Is that roughly clear?

00:56:10.750 --> 00:56:13.210
Because each of them, A
and B are really the things

00:56:13.210 --> 00:56:14.680
that occur more often.

00:56:14.680 --> 00:56:17.080
For A you use one bit,
for B you use two bits.

00:56:17.080 --> 00:56:19.615
So it's on average
1.5 bits per symbol.

00:56:22.450 --> 00:56:25.140
Let's make it precise
and then we'll

00:56:25.140 --> 00:56:26.730
come back to the intuition.

00:56:31.930 --> 00:56:33.070
Expected code length.

00:56:33.070 --> 00:56:35.080
So this is the
measure we will use

00:56:35.080 --> 00:56:39.370
to evaluate all our codes
in the first half basically

00:56:39.370 --> 00:56:40.820
of the course.

00:56:40.820 --> 00:56:42.130
So what is this?

00:56:42.130 --> 00:56:45.770
This is-- one way to think about
it is compressed size divided

00:56:45.770 --> 00:56:48.050
by uncompressed size so
how many bits you are using

00:56:48.050 --> 00:56:53.390
per symbol of input on average.

00:56:53.390 --> 00:56:56.300
So there is an expectation
floating around,

00:56:56.300 --> 00:56:58.188
let's come to that.

00:56:58.188 --> 00:57:00.230
Sometimes it is called
compression rate sometimes

00:57:00.230 --> 00:57:02.180
it is called compression ratio.

00:57:02.180 --> 00:57:04.130
Sometimes people use
compression ratio

00:57:04.130 --> 00:57:06.980
to mean the reciprocal
of this quantity.

00:57:06.980 --> 00:57:09.907
So lots of confusion, don't
worry too much about it.

00:57:09.907 --> 00:57:11.990
Whenever we give you a
homework or a quest problem

00:57:11.990 --> 00:57:16.490
we will clarify what
we mean exactly.

00:57:16.490 --> 00:57:19.730
But really what it means
is the bits per symbol.

00:57:19.730 --> 00:57:21.290
This is the way
to think about it.

00:57:21.290 --> 00:57:23.660
How many bits you are
spending per input symbol.

00:57:26.430 --> 00:57:30.990
So let's say you have a symbol
x with code length l(x).

00:57:30.990 --> 00:57:33.940
So in the previous
slide for example,

00:57:33.940 --> 00:57:37.260
A had a code length of one,
B had a code length of two,

00:57:37.260 --> 00:57:39.700
so that sort of thing.

00:57:39.700 --> 00:57:41.290
And probability P(x).

00:57:41.290 --> 00:57:44.590
Then the expected
length of your--

00:57:44.590 --> 00:57:46.990
of a single symbol is this.

00:57:49.650 --> 00:57:51.200
That should be clear hopefully.

00:57:51.200 --> 00:57:54.440
If it is not-- if you think
you have-- you need to brush up

00:57:54.440 --> 00:57:57.050
on your probability let us we
can find some resources send it

00:57:57.050 --> 00:57:58.190
to you.

00:57:58.190 --> 00:58:00.380
But hopefully this
is very basic stuff,

00:58:00.380 --> 00:58:02.510
hopefully everybody
is comfortable.

00:58:07.480 --> 00:58:10.900
So for this code, let's just
compute the probability.

00:58:14.470 --> 00:58:17.080
So let's write the length down.

00:58:17.080 --> 00:58:21.640
So zero is Length 1 this is
Length 2, Length 3, Length 3.

00:58:21.640 --> 00:58:24.790
Hopefully this is clear.

00:58:24.790 --> 00:58:31.960
And then we just need to do
P(A) times L(A) plus P(B)

00:58:31.960 --> 00:58:37.250
times L(B), and so on.

00:58:37.250 --> 00:58:38.570
Can somebody do it for me?

00:58:42.330 --> 00:58:44.050
1.53

00:58:44.550 --> 00:58:45.240
1.53.

00:58:45.240 --> 00:58:47.070
So a couple of students
correctly answered

00:58:47.070 --> 00:58:52.800
1.53, which if you recall
now is very close to 1.5

00:58:52.800 --> 00:58:55.890
which we expected already
from our back of the envelope

00:58:55.890 --> 00:58:59.010
calculations.

00:58:59.010 --> 00:59:00.120
Here is the full math.

00:59:03.560 --> 00:59:05.000
You will have some
quiz questions

00:59:05.000 --> 00:59:10.400
if you need to brush up,
you will get an opportunity.

00:59:10.400 --> 00:59:12.650
So this is good.

00:59:12.650 --> 00:59:15.860
Calculations aside, we went
from two bits per base--

00:59:15.860 --> 00:59:19.835
to bits per symbol to 1.53 bits
per symbol, so that's good.

00:59:22.340 --> 00:59:25.190
We have one unanswered
question, can you decode it

00:59:25.190 --> 00:59:28.110
which we will answer next time.

00:59:28.110 --> 00:59:31.870
So see this is the
point basically,

00:59:31.870 --> 00:59:32.890
is the code lossless?

00:59:32.890 --> 00:59:41.100
So what I would suggest
for next week, do this,

00:59:41.100 --> 00:59:43.643
I guess let me write
something and then

00:59:43.643 --> 00:59:44.685
I will ask you to decode.

00:59:47.500 --> 00:59:49.930
Don't see me writing it
because otherwise you will

00:59:49.930 --> 00:59:51.730
know what order I wrote it.

00:59:56.920 --> 01:00:04.050
Let's say-- so just
decode this and so next

01:00:04.050 --> 01:00:07.050
lecture we will see if
we are able to decode it,

01:00:07.050 --> 01:00:09.150
and whether everybody
decodes it to the same thing

01:00:09.150 --> 01:00:10.950
or do we get like five
different answers.

01:00:14.730 --> 01:00:17.340
And we'll talk about this in
much more detail next lecture.

01:00:17.340 --> 01:00:20.160
You will understand if you
are able to decode it why you

01:00:20.160 --> 01:00:21.510
are able to decode it exactly.

01:00:21.510 --> 01:00:22.260
There is a reason.

01:00:27.310 --> 01:00:28.900
The other thing you
should have seen

01:00:28.900 --> 01:00:30.400
is that the non
uniform distribution

01:00:30.400 --> 01:00:35.290
we saw it, the 0.49, 0.49, 0.01,
0.01, it was worse in a way

01:00:35.290 --> 01:00:36.760
because you had
two extra symbols.

01:00:36.760 --> 01:00:39.490
It wasn't as simple
as the original one.

01:00:39.490 --> 01:00:43.780
I'm talking about these
two distribution, the A, B.

01:00:43.780 --> 01:00:48.232
And the other one we saw with
the ABCD with the 0.49, 0.49,

01:00:48.232 --> 01:00:52.210
0.01 , 0.01.

01:00:52.210 --> 01:00:54.130
So one thing you
might have noticed

01:00:54.130 --> 01:00:56.640
is these two look very similar.

01:00:56.640 --> 01:01:01.317
And so you would expect that the
optimal code like the best you

01:01:01.317 --> 01:01:02.900
can do for both of
these distributions

01:01:02.900 --> 01:01:05.660
should also be similar,
that's just intuitively.

01:01:05.660 --> 01:01:10.290
So right now we got from
two bits per piece to 1.53.

01:01:10.290 --> 01:01:11.640
That's not really optimal.

01:01:11.640 --> 01:01:13.260
And you will see it
in a few lectures

01:01:13.260 --> 01:01:16.950
how you can actually achieve
the best for this distribution.

01:01:16.950 --> 01:01:18.897
And you might think,
why do we care?

01:01:18.897 --> 01:01:19.980
It's a weird distribution.

01:01:19.980 --> 01:01:22.230
But as you will
see later, when you

01:01:22.230 --> 01:01:24.000
have a video compressor
going on and it

01:01:24.000 --> 01:01:26.580
produces some
intermediate thing,

01:01:26.580 --> 01:01:28.200
that can have any distribution.

01:01:28.200 --> 01:01:30.750
And you need to be able
to encode any distribution

01:01:30.750 --> 01:01:37.110
optimally to get to the
best results basically.

01:01:37.110 --> 01:01:39.540
So that's what we will do
for the next few lectures.

01:01:39.540 --> 01:01:41.850
So we will compute the
optimal compression rate

01:01:41.850 --> 01:01:43.080
for this setting.

01:01:43.080 --> 01:01:46.260
We will show that it's
roughly 1.14 bits per base.

01:01:46.260 --> 01:01:52.170
So it's not quite one bit
per symbol, it's 1.14.

01:01:52.170 --> 01:01:54.630
And very interestingly
we will show that--

01:01:54.630 --> 01:01:57.870
we'll show how to achieve this
and we will show you cannot do

01:01:57.870 --> 01:02:00.840
better than this with any scheme
in the world with infinite

01:02:00.840 --> 01:02:02.400
compute whatever you do.

01:02:02.400 --> 01:02:04.140
If you want lossless
compression,

01:02:04.140 --> 01:02:09.120
this is the best you can do for
that particular distribution.

01:02:09.120 --> 01:02:11.590
That's one of the beauty
of information theory

01:02:11.590 --> 01:02:14.200
that you can prove these
impossibility results.

01:02:14.200 --> 01:02:18.280
Any amount of compute, any
thinking, even Albert Einstein.

01:02:18.280 --> 01:02:20.860
The smartest person on Earth
cannot compress it better

01:02:20.860 --> 01:02:21.580
than--

01:02:21.580 --> 01:02:25.870
because you can prove that you
cannot do better than that.

01:02:25.870 --> 01:02:29.740
So just remember your homework,
decode it and I will see you

01:02:29.740 --> 01:02:30.700
in the next lecture.

01:02:30.700 --> 01:02:31.840
Thank you.

01:02:31.840 --> 01:02:34.410
Happy to answer any questions.

